#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# unset

#% text
# `unset(x)` removes the own value of the symbol `x` and all its rules.

#% text
# `unset(f(p))` removes the one rule of `f` whose left side is `f(p)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `unset(x, y, ...)` and `unset([x, y, ...])` remove several. The arguments are not evaluated.

#% item
# The attributes of the symbol stay (they are its signature, set by decorators or `set_attributes`); `clear_attributes(f, eval(attributes(f)))` removes them too. A built-in function keeps its built-in meaning.

#% item
# The left side of `unset(f(p))` is compared with the rules' left sides up to the names of pattern variables: `unset(f(y_))` removes the rule `f(x_) := ...`. When no rule has that left side the value is `failure(not_defined, f(p))`.

#% item
# `unset` works in the middle of an evaluation: later statements of the same block see the symbol without its definitions.

#% item
# A protected symbol (`protect`) cannot be unset: the value is `failure(protected, x)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
x = 5
x + 1
#% output
#= 6

#% cell
unset(x)
x + 1
#% output
#= 1 + x

#% text
# Rules are removed too:

#% cell
rule sq(y_) := y^2
sq(3)
#% output
#= 9

#% cell
unset(sq)
sq(3)
#% output
#= sq(3)

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# One rule only:

#% cell
rule g(0) := 1
rule g(n_) := 10*n
unset(g(0))
[g(0), g(2)]
#% output
#= [0, 20]

#% text
# Inside a block:

#% cell
{ var_x = 2; unset(var_x); var_x }
#% output
#= var_x

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% cell
rule h(1) := 2
unset(h(2))
#% output
#! failure(not_defined, h(2))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `protect`, `clear_attributes`, `attributes`
